    "response":  " To find the rate at which the volume of water in the conical tank is changing, we start by expressing the volume of a cone in terms of its height and radius. The formula for the volume \\( V \\) of a cone is given by:\n\n\\[ V = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h \\]\n\nwhere \\( r \\) is the radius of the base and \\( h \\) is the height of the water in the tank. Since the tank is conical, the radius and height are proportional. The ratio of the radius to the height of the tank is:\n\n\\[ \\frac{r}{h} = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2} \\]\n\nThis means that at any depth \\( h \\), the radius \\( r \\) is:\n\n\\[ r = \\frac{1}{2} h \\]\n\nSubstituting \\( r = \\frac{1}{2} h \\) into the volume formula, we get:\n\n\\[ V = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\left( \\frac{1}{2} h \\right)^2 h = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\left( \\frac{1}{4} h^2 \\right) h = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\frac{1}{4} h^3 = \\frac{\\pi}{12} h^3 \\]\n\nNext, we need to find the rate of change of the volume with respect to time, \\( \\frac{dV}{dt} \\). We do this by differentiating the volume formula with respect to time \\( t \\):\n\n\\[ \\frac{dV}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt} \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{12} h^3 \\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{12} \\cdot 3h^2 \\cdot \\frac{dh}{dt} = \\frac{\\pi}{4} h^2 \\frac{dh}{dt} \\]\n\nWe are given that the water depth is decreasing at a rate of 0.04 m/min when the water is 2 m deep, so \\( \\frac{dh}{dt} = -0.04 \\) m/min and \\( h = 2 \\) m. Substituting these values into the equation for \\( \\frac{dV}{dt} \\), we get:\n\n\\[ \\frac{dV}{dt} = \\frac{\\pi}{4} (2)^2 (-0.04) = \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\cdot 4 \\cdot (-0.04) = \\pi \\cdot (-0.04) = -0.04\\pi \\]\n\nTherefore, the rate at which the volume of water is changing at that instant is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{-0.04\\pi} \\] m^3/min.",
